  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe threat for thunderstorms will linger across northern \nNebraska, at least through the evening hours. Overnight, ridging\naloft will slowly build northeast of the Four Corners as the \nnorthern stream pattern de- amplifies. Warmer air will push into\nsouthwestern Nebraska Friday with the heat ridge building east \ninto most of the forecast area on Saturday. Looking at the NBM \nprobabilities for highs > 100 degrees for North Platte Friday \nthrough Monday, the probabilities of exceedance have come down \nsomewhat from last night's forecast (on the order of 10 to 20%).\nNo surprise, the latest NBM has highs peaking just under 100 \ndegrees in SW Nebraska on Saturday and Sunday. In light of the \ndeterministic GFS and statistical MEX guidance which is warmer \nwith highs Saturday and Sunday well above 100 degrees Saturday \nand Sunday (105 and 104 respectively for North Platte), and the \nEC guidance which has 92 and 97 for highs, feel the NBM forecast\nis a good compromise as it meets these two guidance members in \nthe middle. That being said, with readings approaching 100 \ndegrees Saturday and Sunday, will continue to message heat \npotential. By late Sunday, a northern stream shortwave will \ntraverse southern Canada. This feature will attempt to force a \nfrontal boundary into South Dakota and possibly far northern \nNebraska. The forecast initialized with some slight chance pops \nover northern and central Nebraska Sunday night and this seems \nreasonable given the potential for the front to lie over \nnorthern Nebraska. However, forecast confidence in pcpn over \ncentral and southern areas is not high ATTM. Better chances for \nprecipitation will arrive on Monday with better model agreement \non the front being anchored over northern Nebraska. On Tuesday, \nan upper level low will drop south from western Hudson Bay into \nwestern Ontario. This will force the frontal boundary further \nsouth bringing cooler temperatures to northern Nebraska Tuesday.\nThis will also bring a better chance for precipitation to the \nforecast area Tuesday into Tuesday night.",
